  "problem": "Expressed as a mixed number in its lowest terms, $1\\,\\frac{4}{5}\\times 3\\,\\frac{1}{2}=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{4}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{(1\\times 5)+4}{5}=\\frac{9}{5}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{5}\\times \\frac{7}{2}=\\frac{9\\times 7}{5\\times 2}=\\frac{63}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{63}{10}=6\\,\\text{R}3=6\\,\\frac{3}{10}\n$$",
